"""Extra depth for innovation reports and Colour hub articles (Oct 2026). Inserted before closing CTA / sources. Add only."""

def _innov(problem, regions, questions, claims, verdict):
    return [
        ('h2', 'The problem it solves'), ('p', problem),
        ('h2', 'Relevance by region'), ('ul', regions),
        ('h2', 'Questions to ask suppliers'), ('ul', questions),
        ('h2', 'Claims and labelling'), ('p', claims),
        ('h2', 'Forecut verdict'), ('p', verdict),
    ]


MORE = {
'innovation-plant-based-stretch': _innov(
    'Stretch is now expected in jeans, leggings, swimwear and many shirts, so even a small percentage of elastane is used across billions of garments. Replacing part of that fossil-based content is one of the few sustainability changes that does not affect fit, comfort or appearance.',
    ['<b>Europe:</b> buyers ask for lower-impact materials and documented claims; a strong fit for export denim and swimwear.', '<b>North America:</b> large activewear and denim markets where stretch is standard.', '<b>Asia:</b> major denim and knit manufacturing hubs can adopt it without new machinery.', '<b>Southern hemisphere:</b> swimwear brands in Australia and Brazil are natural early adopters.'],
    ['What share of the stretch fibre is plant-based, and how is it verified?', 'Is the price premium per metre fixed or volume-based?', 'Are stretch and recovery identical to your current quality in your own tests?', 'Which certifications or life cycle data can you share?'],
    'Plant-based content applies to the stretch fibre only, which is usually a small percentage of the garment. Say "stretch fibre made with 70% plant-based content" rather than calling the garment plant-based or bio-based.',
    'A low-risk upgrade. Test it on one high-volume stretch style; if fit and recovery match, it can become the default for that quality.'),
'innovation-water-resistance-in-the-yarn': _innov(
    'Topical water-repellent finishes wear off with washing and, when fluorinated, raise environmental concerns. Building resistance into the yarn aims to make performance longer-lasting and free of PFAS chemistry.',
    ['<b>Northern Europe, the UK, Japan and the Pacific Northwest:</b> rainwear and commuter outerwear are core categories.', '<b>South and Southeast Asia:</b> monsoon wear, uniforms and travel trousers.', '<b>Outdoor markets worldwide:</b> brands are replacing fluorinated finishes across ranges.'],
    ['What water-repellency rating is achieved before and after 10 and 20 washes?', 'Is the yarn available in recycled versions?', 'Can it be combined with your current fabric constructions?', 'Can you provide PFAS test reports from an accredited lab?'],
    'Use measured results ("water-resistant to [standard] after 20 washes") and state "PFAS-free" only with lab results. Avoid "waterproof" unless the garment, including seams, passes a waterproofness test.',
    'Important for any brand selling water-resistant products. Start testing for AW27 outerwear; expect buyers to require PFAS-free options more often over the next few seasons.'),
'innovation-elastane-free-natural-stretch': _innov(
    'Blending elastane into wool, cotton or silk makes garments harder to recycle and can reduce their lifespan as the elastane ages. Mechanical stretch keeps the fabric in one fibre family while still giving comfort.',
    ['<b>Europe:</b> premium tailoring and the push for recyclable mono-material products.', '<b>Japan and Korea:</b> clients who value natural fibres and refined comfort.', '<b>India and the Gulf:</b> travel suiting and wedding menswear that must be comfortable for long events.', '<b>North America:</b> commuter and work-from-anywhere tailoring.'],
    ['What stretch percentage and recovery does the fabric achieve?', 'How does it behave after pressing and steaming?', 'Is the fabric available in lighter weights for warm climates?', 'What is the minimum order for development colours?'],
    'Describe it as "natural mechanical stretch" or "stretch from the weave", and state the fibre content (for example "100% wool"). Do not compare stretch levels with elastane fabrics unless you have tested both.',
    'A premium story with steady growth. Best for tailoring and suiting brands that want comfort without a technical look.'),
'innovation-waterless-denim-finishing': _innov(
    'Denim washing can use large volumes of water and chemicals per garment. Laser, ozone and low-water technologies reduce this while giving similar looks, and they can also reduce worker exposure to harsh chemicals.',
    ['<b>South Asia (India, Pakistan, Bangladesh):</b> major denim producers in water-stressed regions.', '<b>Turkey and North Africa:</b> fast-turnaround suppliers to European brands.', '<b>Mexico and Central America:</b> denim supply for North American brands.', '<b>Consumers in Europe and North America:</b> increasingly look for documented low-impact denim.'],
    ['What is the water, energy and chemical use per garment for each wash?', 'Which looks can be achieved with laser and ozone alone?', 'Can you provide data from a recognised measurement tool?', 'How consistent are results across bulk production?'],
    'Use figures you can document ("uses up to X% less water than our previous wash") and state the comparison. Avoid "zero water" unless the full process uses none.',
    'Already scaling. Make it the default for hero washes and use the data in product storytelling.'),
'innovation-fibre-traceability': _innov(
    'Supply chains for clothing are long and often hidden. Traceability links the finished product back to the farm or source, which supports quality claims, regulatory compliance and responsible sourcing.',
    ['<b>European Union:</b> the Digital Product Passport will make traceability data a market requirement for textiles over the coming years.', '<b>United States:</b> import rules and buyer due-diligence programmes require proof of origin for some fibres.', '<b>Asia and Africa:</b> suppliers that can share traceability data gain an edge with international buyers.', '<b>Domestic markets everywhere:</b> provenance stories (handloom, organic, regional wool) build trust.'],
    ['Which traceability platforms do you already use?', 'Can you provide batch-level data from fibre to fabric?', 'Which certifications cover each step?', 'How is the data shared: QR code, platform login or documents?'],
    'Only claim what the data shows. "Traceable to the spinning mill" is different from "traceable to the farm". Make clear which steps are covered.',
    'Essential for exporters and increasingly valuable for premium domestic brands. Start with your top-selling styles and expand.'),
'innovation-long-fibre-european-hemp': _innov(
    'Most hemp has been processed for short fibres, giving coarse fabrics. Long-fibre processing aims to produce finer, smoother yarns that could compete with linen, from a crop that needs relatively little water and few inputs.',
    ['<b>Europe:</b> building a local supply chain, with early premium applications.', '<b>Mediterranean, Australia and South America:</b> warm-climate markets where linen-like fabrics sell well.', '<b>South and Southeast Asia:</b> heat-friendly summer shirting and home textiles once supply grows.'],
    ['Is the fabric 100% hemp or a hemp-linen or hemp-cotton blend?', 'What is the yarn count and fabric weight available?', 'Where was the hemp grown and processed?', 'What are realistic volumes and prices for 2027 and 2028?'],
    'State the blend precisely and avoid broad environmental claims unless supported by data for the specific supply chain.',
    'A pilot to watch. Expect blends first; plan a small capsule once supply and pricing stabilise.'),
'innovation-biodegradable-biopolyester': _innov(
    'Polyester is durable and cheap but persists in the environment and sheds microfibres. A bio-based polymer that performs like polyester but breaks down or recycles more easily could address both issues.',
    ['<b>Global sportswear:</b> polyester-heavy categories that face growing pressure on microplastics.', '<b>Europe:</b> regulations and buyer policies focusing on microplastics and end of life.', '<b>Asia:</b> large polyester manufacturing bases that could adopt materials compatible with existing lines.'],
    ['Under what conditions does it biodegrade, and has that been independently tested?', 'Can it run on our current spinning and knitting lines?', 'What is the expected price compared with virgin and recycled polyester?', 'When will commercial volumes be available?'],
    'Do not claim "biodegradable" without certified tests that state the conditions (industrial compost, soil or marine) and timeframe. Many markets restrict vague biodegradability claims.',
    'Promising but early. Track partners and pilot results through 2027 before planning products.'),
'innovation-mono-material-thread': _innov(
    'Garments made from several materials are hard to recycle, because fibres must be separated. Threads, labels and trims are a common source of mixed materials, so matching them to the main fabric helps recycling.',
    ['<b>Europe:</b> ecodesign and recycling rules increasingly reward single-material products.', '<b>India and South Asia:</b> embroidery-heavy cotton and viscose products could become more recyclable.', '<b>Global basics:</b> T-shirts, shirts and home textiles are the easiest place to start.'],
    ['What is the seam strength compared with polyester thread?', 'Does the thread perform on high-speed sewing and embroidery machines?', 'Which colours are available and how colour-fast are they?', 'What is the price difference per garment?'],
    'Say "sewn with cellulose thread" or "designed as a mono-material garment" only if the fabric, thread, labels and trims all belong to the same fibre family.',
    'A practical step towards recyclable design. Test it on simple cotton styles first.'),
'innovation-recycled-plastic-embellishment': _innov(
    'Embellishment uses large quantities of small plastic parts, usually made from new material. Using recycled plastic reduces virgin plastic use and gives a strong story for statement pieces.',
    ['<b>India:</b> a global centre for embroidery and embellishment, well placed to supply recycled options.', '<b>Europe and the UK:</b> buyers looking for circular stories in eveningwear and occasion wear.', '<b>Middle East:</b> embellished eveningwear and bridal could adopt recycled sequins for capsule lines.'],
    ['What share of the material is recycled, and how is it verified?', 'Are colour, shine and durability equal to standard sequins?', 'Can the embellishment withstand dry cleaning or washing?', 'What are the minimum order quantities?'],
    'Use specific wording ("sequins made from 100% recycled PET") and keep supplier certificates. The garment as a whole is not "recycled" if only the embellishment is.',
    'A strong capsule story for 2027. Start with one hero piece to test customer response.'),
'colour-theory-for-fashion': [
 ('h2', 'Temperature and light'),
 ('p', 'Colours are often described as warm (reds, oranges, yellows) or cool (blues, greens, violets), but every hue can lean either way: a blue-red feels cooler than an orange-red. Light changes colour too. Daylight, warm indoor light and store LED lighting can make the same fabric look different, so palettes should be checked under the lighting customers will see.'),
 ('h2', 'Colour and fabric'),
 ('p', 'Fabric texture changes how a colour reads. A colour on satin looks brighter and darker in the folds; the same colour on linen looks softer and lighter. Knits absorb light and look deeper. When you build a palette, view it on the actual fabrics of the collection.'),
 ('h2', 'Proportion'),
 ('p', 'The amount of each colour matters as much as the choice. A common approach is to use a dominant colour in about 60% of an outfit or display, a secondary colour in about 30% and an accent in about 10%. This keeps outfits balanced and makes it easier for customers to combine pieces.'),
 ('h2', 'Colour across regions'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Hot, bright climates:</b> strong sunlight supports saturated colour and white.', '<b>Northern climates:</b> softer, muted palettes often read better in grey daylight.', '<b>Cultural meaning:</b> colours carry different associations; white is linked with weddings in some cultures and mourning in others. Check meanings for each market.']),
 ('h2', 'Practical exercise'),
 ('p', 'Take one season palette and create three outfits: a tonal look, a neutral-plus-accent look and a complementary look. Photograph them together. The exercise shows quickly which colours carry the collection and which need a supporting role.'),
],
'colour-pantone-tcx-guide': [
 ('h2', 'Other colour systems you will meet'),
 ('ul', ['<b>TPG:</b> Pantone\'s paper-based fashion and home references, useful for print and packaging.', '<b>Pantone Solid Coated and Uncoated:</b> print inks, not textile standards.', '<b>RGB and HEX:</b> screen colours used for websites and digital design.', '<b>Mill or dye-house standards:</b> some suppliers use their own colour cards.', '<b>Spectrophotometer data:</b> numeric colour measurements (often CIELAB) used to compare dips objectively.']),
 ('h2', 'Understanding colour tolerance'),
 ('p', 'No dye lot matches a standard perfectly. Brands set a tolerance: how close a dip or bulk shipment must be to pass. Instrument-based measurement gives a number for the difference, which is more consistent than visual judgement alone. Agree on the tolerance and method with suppliers before production.'),
 ('h2', 'Metamerism'),
 ('p', 'Two colours that match under one light can look different under another. This is called metamerism. It is common when different fibres or dyes are used, for example a cotton top and a polyester skirt meant to match. Checking under at least two light sources, such as daylight and store lighting, catches most problems.'),
 ('h2', 'Approving colour for multiple regions'),
 ('p', 'If you produce the same style in different factories or countries, use the same physical standard, the same light source and the same tolerance everywhere. Keep approved standards in good condition, away from light and moisture, and replace them when they age.'),
 ('h2', 'A simple colour file for each season'),
 ('ul', ['Colour name used in the business.', 'Pantone TCX reference.', 'Approved standard for each fabric base.', 'Approved lab dip codes per supplier.', 'Notes on fibre-specific adjustments.']),
],
'colour-indian-festive-colours': [
 ('h2', 'Regional colour traditions'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Kerala:</b> ivory and gold kasavu for Onam and weddings.', '<b>Bengal:</b> red-bordered white sarees (laal paar) for Durga Puja.', '<b>Maharashtra:</b> the nine colours of Navratri are widely followed, with a colour for each day.', '<b>Rajasthan and Gujarat:</b> bright pinks, oranges and greens, with leheriya and bandhani patterns.', '<b>Punjab:</b> vivid phulkari colours and festive pinks and yellows.', '<b>South India:</b> rich silk colours such as temple reds, mango yellows and peacock blues.']),
 ('h2', 'Designing a festive range'),
 ('p', 'Start with the festival calendar for your customers and choose two or three hero colours for each window: for example jewel tones and gold for Diwali, varied brights for Navratri, ivory and gold for Onam. Add a set of pastels and neutrals for customers who prefer a softer look.'),
 ('h2', 'Metallics and finishes'),
 ('p', 'Gold remains central, but the shade has shifted: antique gold, champagne and rose gold now sit alongside bright yellow gold. Silver and gunmetal work with cooler palettes. Matte zari and tonal metallic threads give a quieter festive look.'),
 ('h2', 'Festive colour beyond India'),
 ('p', 'South Asian communities in the UK, North America, the Gulf, Southeast Asia and Australia celebrate the same festivals and look for the same colour cues, often in silhouettes that also suit local events. Other celebrations follow similar logic: red and gold for Lunar New Year, emerald, gold and white for Eid, and red, green and gold for Christmas. A colour strategy built around the calendar works in every market.'),
 ('h2', 'Respecting meaning'),
 ('p', 'Colour associations vary by region and community and are changing over time. When in doubt, ask customers, use clear product descriptions and offer choice rather than assuming one tradition.'),
],
'colour-skin-tones': [
 ('h2', 'Undertones in practice'),
 ('p', 'Undertone is the subtle hue beneath the surface of the skin. A simple guide: if gold jewellery tends to look better, the undertone may be warm; if silver looks better, it may be cool; if both work, it may be neutral. These are tendencies, not rules, and personal taste matters most.'),
 ('h2', 'Colours that work broadly'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Jewel tones</b> such as emerald, sapphire, ruby and amethyst flatter a wide range of skin tones.', '<b>Soft whites and ivories</b> are often easier to wear than stark optical white.', '<b>Deep neutrals</b> such as navy, charcoal and chocolate work as alternatives to black.', '<b>Mid-tone versions</b> of trend colours are usually more wearable than the most saturated versions.']),
 ('h2', 'Contrast and value'),
 ('p', 'The difference between skin, hair and eye colour is called personal contrast. People with high contrast often look striking in strong colour combinations; people with low contrast may prefer tonal looks. Offering both strong and tonal outfits in a range helps more customers find their look.'),
 ('h2', 'Designing for global customers'),
 ('p', 'Customers around the world have a wide range of skin tones and undertones. Brands selling internationally should test key colours on a diverse group of fit models and in photography, so product images help customers choose well. This also reduces returns caused by colour disappointment.'),
 ('h2', 'Inclusive product photography'),
 ('ul', ['Show each hero colour on at least two or three different skin tones.', 'Use consistent, colour-accurate lighting.', 'Add close-up fabric shots in daylight.', 'Describe colours with clear names and comparisons ("a warm mid-pink").']),
 ('h2', 'Nude and skin-tone basics'),
 ('p', 'Products sold as "nude" or "skin" colours, such as lingerie, hosiery, footwear and linings, should be offered in a range of shades, not just one. Many brands now offer five or more shades for these categories.'),
],
'colour-building-a-colour-story': [
 ('h2', '6. Name and describe each colour'),
 ('p', 'Give every colour a short, memorable name that works for customers, such as "Monsoon Moss" or "Clay Pink", alongside its internal code and TCX reference. Use the same name in tech packs, product listings and marketing so the story stays consistent.'),
 ('h2', '7. Check colour across categories'),
 ('p', 'The same colour can look different in knitwear, denim, footwear and accessories. Review all categories together so a customer can build a full outfit. Small adjustments per fabric are normal; the goal is a family that looks intentional.'),
 ('h2', '8. Plan by region and season'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Southern hemisphere:</b> the northern spring palette usually arrives in stores there about six months later.', '<b>Hot climates:</b> lighter, brighter versions often sell better in summer.', '<b>Festival and holiday windows:</b> add seasonal accents for Diwali, Eid, Lunar New Year or Christmas.', '<b>Cooler markets:</b> deeper versions of the same colours extend the story into autumn.']),
 ('h2', '9. Learn from sales'),
 ('p', 'After the season, compare sales by colour with your plan. Note which colours sold out, which needed markdowns and which worked only in certain categories. This history makes the next colour story more accurate.'),
 ('h2', 'Example: a six-colour capsule'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Core:</b> navy and ecru.', '<b>Fashion:</b> luminous blue and clay pink.', '<b>Neutral:</b> stone and chocolate.', '<b>Accent (small quantities):</b> marigold for trims and accessories.']),
],
}

# --- Depth added 31 Oct 2026 (weekly content-depth pass): practical guidance appended to the thinnest pages. Add only. ---
MORE['innovation-recycled-plastic-embellishment'] = MORE['innovation-recycled-plastic-embellishment'] + [
 ('h2', 'How to use it in a collection'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Start with placement, not all-over work.</b> A recycled-sequin yoke, cuff or hem border on an otherwise plain dress tests customer response at a fraction of the cost of a fully embellished gown.',
         '<b>Choose the hero piece carefully.</b> Evening tops, occasion skirts and festive blouses sell on surface; they are the best place to tell the recycled story.',
         '<b>Match the base fabric.</b> Lightweight embellishment suits sheer organza, tulle and georgette; heavier three-dimensional flowers need a stable base such as crepe, satin or a fused panel.',
         '<b>Plan care instructions early.</b> Recycled PET sequins can behave differently under heat, so test pressing, steaming and dry cleaning before bulk.']),
 ('h2', 'Reading it by region'),
 ('p', 'In <b>India and the Gulf</b>, where festive and wedding wear relies on sequins, beads and mirror work, the opportunity is volume: a single karigar workshop can switch suppliers without changing technique. In <b>Europe</b>, buyers will ask for documentation first, so keep certificates and percentages ready. In <b>North America</b>, recycled embellishment works best in party and holiday capsules where shoppers already accept a premium. In <b>Africa</b>, Lagos and Johannesburg designers who already work with beadwork and fringe can use it as a craft-plus-circularity story. In <b>Oceania</b>, race-day and summer occasion wear are the natural testing ground.'),
 ('h2', 'A simple test plan'),
 ('p', 'Order a sampling quantity of recycled sequins or flowers in two of your core evening colours. Make one placement style and one heavier style, wear-test both for a full evening, wash or dry clean them as you would in store, and compare shine, colour and loss of pieces with your current material. If the results match, move one best-selling style across for the next season and tell customers exactly which part is recycled.'),
]
MORE['innovation-biodegradable-biopolyester'] = MORE['innovation-biodegradable-biopolyester'] + [
 ('h2', 'Where it fits first'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Linings and pocketing:</b> hidden components with simple performance needs are the lowest-risk trial.',
         '<b>Short-life categories:</b> event merchandise, uniforms with take-back schemes and single-season promotional pieces.',
         '<b>Blends you already struggle to recycle:</b> where a polyester component blocks recycling, a material designed for chemical recycling or controlled biodegradation can simplify end-of-life options.',
         '<b>Not yet:</b> high-performance outerwear, swimwear and activewear where durability, chlorine or UV resistance and colour fastness must match conventional polyester.']),
 ('h2', 'Reading it by region'),
 ('p', 'The <b>European Union</b> is tightening rules on green claims, so a biodegradable fibre will only help if the test conditions and timeframe can be stated clearly. In <b>North America</b>, some states restrict "biodegradable" on product labels, so marketing should focus on what has been certified. In <b>South and Southeast Asia</b>, where most polyester garments are made, mills are the right partners for early trials because they can run the fibre on existing lines. In <b>Africa and South America</b>, where much end-of-life clothing ends up, a material that breaks down in real-world conditions would matter most, but evidence for that is still limited.'),
 ('h2', 'What to ask before you commit'),
 ('p', 'Ask the supplier for the certified test standard, the conditions (industrial compost, home compost, soil or marine), the time to break down and whether any additives or dyes change the result. Ask how the fabric performs after washing and in storage, because a fibre designed to break down must not degrade in a warehouse. Treat early pilots as learning projects and keep conventional or recycled polyester as the default until results are proven over a full season.'),
]
